SC["a5c27_door"]={img:"door",
 t:()=>SYS(`ACT FIVE — THE PATTERN SAGE · CHAPTER TWENTY-SEVEN — THE DOOR BETWEEN`)+P(`The center of the Grand Loom is the only place in the world where the world has an EDGE.`)+P(`Suyin walks you in along the master warp — two Pattern Sages on a bridge of light a quarter-million lines wide — to the place where every thread of the weave turns back on itself: the selvage, the woven border, the hem of heaven. Standing at it is like standing at the rail of a ship the size of everything. Beyond the last line there is no dark, no light, no distance. There is the BETWEEN, and you have been here before.`)+P(`“The books say a Sage can step off the cloth and stand on the loom-frame itself,” Suyin says. Her voice is steady; her hands are not, quite. “I never dared. I was woven: a woven line that leaves the cloth is just CUT. But you, stitch. You were sewn in from outside. Your thread runs THROUGH the selvage. It always has. Follow your own line off the edge, and come back, and tell me what the far side of the sky smells like.”`)+P(`You look down at your own gold, and follow it with the new seeing — through your chest, back, out, OFF the world — and step.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Off the edge of the cloth.`,go:"a5c27_between"}]};

SC["a5c27_between"]={t:()=>P(`The Loom Between Worlds. The not-place. You died once and drifted through it, a loose stitch, calm as the dead are calm. Now you stand IN it, alive, awake, a Pattern Sage on the loom-frame of everything, and the seeing the realm gave you finally has room to open all the way.`)+P(`Weaves beyond counting hang in the dark like aurora: worlds, each one a bolt of woven light, near and far in a geometry that has nothing to do with distance. Yours — the mountain's, the river's, Mei's and Bao's and Tang's — hangs closest, vast, pale-dawn-colored, the Great Fray a dark bruise along one border with a tiny constellation of held tension blazing at its center, four hundred years of one woman's stubbornness visible from OUTSIDE CREATION. You stand a while and look at it, because no one has ever seen it, and someone should.`)+P(`And there, off the bruise's edge, runs a single gold thread. Yours. Out of your chest, off your world's cloth, across the dark, to a smaller weave, further off, the color of sodium lights and rain. Earth. The line is whole. The line was always whole. <i>The Loom remembers its stitches</i>: you have heard it as threat and as comfort and as theology, and here, finally, is what it MEANS: nothing that was ever woven in is ever fully cut out. The door home isn't a door. It's your own thread, and you are still on the far end of it, and the far end is still on you.`)+P(`You follow it with your eyes only, to where it meets the rain-colored weave. The entry-point glows, very small, very stubborn: a porch light burning on the hem of a world.`)+((G.f.bg=="sister")?(P(`You would know that knot anywhere. You tied its other half when you were nine. It is not a metaphor and never was: someone on the far side keeps the line warm, nightly, as you once kept it, and the work of it has frayed her edges and she has kept on doing it, for years, into the dark, on no evidence at all. The Between is very quiet. You stand on the frame of everything with your hand over the line in your chest, and you let it hurt exactly as much as it does.`)):("")),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Something else moves in the dark. Look.`,go:"a5c27_stitches"}]};

SC["a5c27_stitches"]={t:()=>P(`Loose stitches.`)+P(`They drift through the Between the way you once drifted: souls off their looms, between cloths, calm with the calm you remember: dozens in sight at any moment, sparks of every color, falling slowly toward the weaves that will catch them. You watch one — a small blue-white life off some weave you'll never know — drift, touch the dawn-colored cloth of YOUR world, and sink in, gently, the Between sewing it down with a motion you recognize from the inside.`)+P(`And you see where it lands: the bruise. The Fray's edge. EXACTLY the fray's edge. The next one likewise, and the one after. And the seeing finally hands you the whole mechanism, simple as a kitchen rule: the Between sews loose stitches WHERE THE CLOTH IS THINNEST. Of course it does. That is what mending is. Every transmigrator in four hundred years — the Frayed One in her marsh, the sleepers in their hammocks, the gold-haired boy on a riverbank — every single one was sewn into the torn province, because every single one was SENT THERE, the way thread is sent to a hole.`)+P(`The Court has been harvesting the patches. Four centuries, on the theory that foreign souls fray the weave — and the arrow ran backward the whole time: the fraying CALLS them. You will need this said in front of the Lady, and said with proof a clerk can check. But standing here, on the frame, watching the Between do its patient needlework, you already know it, hand-deep, past argument.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Stay out on the frame and watch ten more souls land, counting, until knowing becomes EVIDENCE you can recite.`,do:()=>{addIns(1);note("+1 Insight. Ten souls, ten thin places, ten exact landings \u2014 you stand on the loom-frame of everything tallying heaven's needlework like Qiu tallying rice, and by the tenth you can recite the mechanism cold. \"They're not wounds,\" you tell Suyin, back on the bridge. \"They're SUTURES. We arrive where the sky is thinnest because we were SENT.\" She is quiet a long time. \"Stitch \u2014 we are going to need the Lady sitting down.\"");},go:"a5c27_return"},
  {id:"c1",l:`Step back now. The Between's work is not a thing to spectate, and Suyin has been alone on the bridge too long.`,do:()=>{G.heart++;note("+1 Heart. You carry the knowing back unproven and tell it to Suyin plainly: not wounds \u2014 sutures, sent to the tearing. She stands very still. \"Forty years the Court called me a loose stitch it had caught,\" she says at last. \"And I was the thread heaven sent to the hole.\" Proof can wait for the index. Some sentences shouldn't wait at all.");},go:"a5c27_return"}]};

SC["a5c27_return"]={t:()=>P(`You come back along the master warp in the changed light of having seen the world from off it, and the city above is the same and is never going to look the same.`)+P(`Suyin walks beside you in silence most of the way. At the gallery stair she stops, and when she speaks, the silk in her voice has a seam showing.`)+P(`“On the far side. The smaller weave, rain-colored.” She does not look at you. “Your line still runs to it. I felt it through the bridge when you stepped: the whole master warp felt it, child, you are STRUNG between two heavens and you walk around all day as if your shoes simply fit.” A breath. “When the new pattern is tied and the sky is safe: that line will bear a Sage's weight. You could walk it. Home. You have been able to walk it, I suspect, since the hour you broke through.” Now she looks at you. “I am not raising the question to answer it. I am raising it because you have six days, and a man should not meet that door at a run.”`)+SYS(`The door is real, the thread is whole, and it will bear a Sage's weight. Six days to the crossover.`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Down into the city, where the rota drills and the people you tied are waiting.`,go:"a5c28_debt"}]};